               <t indent="0" pn="section-4.2.7.5.3-1">Once the stage-1 index I1 and the stage-2 residual res_Q10[] have
@@ -3980,24 +3443,22 @@
 using the low-complexity Inverse Harmonic Mean Weighting (IHMW)
 function proposed in <xref target="LAROIA-ICASSP" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="LAROIA-ICASSP"/>.  The weights are derived
 directly from the stage-1 codebook vector.  Let cb1_Q8[k] be the k'th
-entry of the stage-1 codebook vector from Table 23 or Table 24.
+entry of the stage-1 codebook vector from <xref target="table-23" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="Table 23"/> or <xref target="table-24" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="Table 24"/>.
 Then, for 0 &lt;= k &lt; d_LPC, the following expression computes the
 square of the weight as a Q18 value:</t>
-              <t indent="0" pn="section-4.2.7.5.3-3"><artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt=""><![CDATA[
+              <artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt="" pn="section-4.2.7.5.3-3"><![CDATA[
          w2_Q18[k] = (1024/(cb1_Q8[k] - cb1_Q8[k-1])
                       + 1024/(cb1_Q8[k+1] - cb1_Q8[k])) << 16
 ]]></artwork>
-</t>
               <t indent="0" pn="section-4.2.7.5.3-4">where cb1_Q8[-1] = 0 and cb1_Q8[d_LPC] = 256, and the division is
 integer division.  This is reduced to an unsquared, Q9 value using
 the following square-root approximation:</t>
-              <t indent="0" pn="section-4.2.7.5.3-5"><artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt=""><![CDATA[
+              <artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt="" pn="section-4.2.7.5.3-5"><![CDATA[
               i = ilog(w2_Q18[k])
               f = (w2_Q18[k]>>(i-8)) & 127
               y = ((i&1) ? 32768 : 46214) >> ((32-i)>>1)
               w_Q9[k] = y + ((213*f*y)>>16)
 ]]></artwork>
-</t>
               <t indent="0" pn="section-4.2.7.5.3-6">The constant 46214 here is approximately the square root of 2 in Q15.
 The cb1_Q8[] vector completely determines these weights, and they may
 be tabulated and stored as 13-bit unsigned values (with a range of
@@ -4007,313 +3468,300 @@
